Hackedbox Startup Directory

Hackedbox provides a simple startup directory for programs that should be started when the Hackedbox session begins.

The startup system is intentionally simple. There is no special configuration language and no numbered startup files. Everything in the startup directory is a normal shell script.

How It Works:
When Hackedbox starts, hackedbox-session checks:
~/.hackedbox/startup/
Every executable file in that directory is run.
Startup files are ordinary shell scripts.

For example:
    #!/bin/sh

    volumeicon &

Save this as:
~/.hackedbox/startup/sound

Then make it executable:
chmod +x ~/.hackedbox/startup/sound

this will send the volume icon to the icon menu (middle click)

User Programs
The startup directory is also a convenient place to start programs automatically when Hackedbox starts.

For example:
    #!/bin/sh

    conky &
    xclock &
    my-program &

Save it as:
~/.hackedbox/startup/programs

and make it executable:
chmod +x ~/.hackedbox/startup/programs

Anything that can normally be started from a terminal can be placed in a startup script.

Startup Programs and System Services:
The Hackedbox startup directory is not a replacement for the system init system.
OpenRC, runit, dinit, s6, systemd, or another init system should continue to handle system services. The Hackedbox startup directory is intended for graphical-session programs and user-specific startup tasks.

For example:
    volume/tray applications
    network tray applications
    desktop utilities
    notification programs
    user applications
    graphical configuration programs
    scripts needed by the user's desktop session

This allows Hackedbox to work with different init systems without requiring Hackedbox to manage those services itself.

Keep It Simple:
The startup system follows one rule:
If it is an executable shell script in ~/.hackedbox/startup/, Hackedbox will run it when the session starts. There are no startup numbers, no complicated dependency system, and no Hackedbox-specific scripting language. Just normal shell scripts in one directory.














